# {{.Project}}

An opinionated gin-kit application backed by the versioned runtime.

## Development

```bash
go mod tidy
gin-kit run
```

The runtime lives in the versioned `github.com/Alfian57/gin-kit/runtime`
dependency. Application routes, domains, services, and middleware belong in
this repository and can be customized without editing runtime internals.

## Configuration

Copy `.env.example` to `.env`, then configure the database and environment.
The application loads `.env` at startup through the runtime's typed
configuration: real environment variables always win over `.env` values,
malformed values fail startup, and outside development `DATABASE_URL` and
`CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` are required. Timeouts use Go duration syntax (`10s`).

Generate secrets with `openssl rand -base64 48` (for example `JWT_SECRET`).
Run `gin-kit doctor` for actionable diagnostics.

## Background work

Job handlers live in `internal/jobs` and are registered explicitly in
`app.New`. The default `QUEUE_DRIVER=sync` executes jobs inline; set
`QUEUE_DRIVER=redis` with `REDIS_URL` (the Docker Compose file ships a Redis
service) for persistent jobs with retries and a supervised worker. Add
cron-style tasks with `runtime/schedule` and register the scheduler with
`application.Go("scheduler", scheduler.Run)` — single-instance per deployment.
The cache is available through `application.Cache()` (`CACHE_DRIVER=memory`
or `redis`).

{{if .Auth}}## Authentication

Ready-to-use JSON authentication backed by the `users` and `refresh_tokens`
tables (run `gin-kit db up` first, and set a 32+ byte `JWT_SECRET`):

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/register` | Create an account and sign in |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/login` | Exchange credentials for tokens |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/refresh` | Rotate a single-use refresh token |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` | Revoke a refresh token |
| `GET /api/v1/me` | The authenticated account (Bearer token) |

Passwords use Argon2id; refresh tokens are stored hashed and rotate on every
use.

{{end}}## API documentation

The API documents itself — no annotations. Open `/docs` in development for
Swagger UI (`DOCS_ENABLED` defaults to true only in development); the spec at
`/openapi.json` is generated from the live routes plus the typed descriptions
the generators emit. Configure title, version, servers, and optional basic
auth through the `DOCS_*` variables in `.env`.

## Mail, WhatsApp, and storage

Send email with `mail.New(cfg.MailOptions())` — the default `MAIL_DRIVER=log`
renders messages into the logs, and `docker compose --profile mail up` starts
Mailpit for a local inbox. Store files with
`storage.New(cfg.StorageOptions())` — `STORAGE_DRIVER=local` keeps files
under `./storage` with strict path confinement, `s3` targets any
S3-compatible store, and `docker compose --profile storage up` starts MinIO.

Send an approved WhatsApp Business template with the official Meta Cloud API:

```go
client, err := whatsapp.New(cfg.WhatsAppOptions())
if err != nil {
    return err
}
defer client.Close()

err = client.Send(ctx, whatsapp.NewAuthenticationMessage(
    "+62812345678", "login_code", "id", code,
))
```

`WHATSAPP_DRIVER=log` is the safe default and logs only redacted delivery
metadata. Set `WHATSAPP_DRIVER=cloud` plus the Meta access token, phone number
ID, and Graph API version to deliver messages. The approved template name and
language are always explicit at the call site.
